{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot-js/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-21T23:54:25.912481Z","id":"6fc3621234e568b808a37fbbd32398d59a3ba096646a75fbea60c7db148c4084","body":{"detail":"ThreadCpuClock uses cpu-time's ThreadTime and, when ThreadTime::try_now fails, wall time from the engine thread's start: on such a platform a preempted or loaded machine charges the script for time it did not run. The interrupt handler (lib.rs set_interrupt_handler) runs only every few thousand QuickJS instructions, so a single long native builtin is not stopped until it returns, and the outcome's cpu_ms can overshoot Limits::cpu_ms by that much. Measured on macOS only; Linux has not run the conformance suite's CPU cases.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":64},"end":{"line":76},"content_hash":"85c07686257da5d9a959b397e9d4ccb6e2596bc1c80ba100c00f5fb00d65c884"},"summary":"The CPU budget falls back to wall time where the thread CPU clock is missing, and is checked only between interpreter steps","tags":["session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot-js/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-21T23:54:25.912534Z","id":"6785a4671017f5428b94a43f9381ddfd2c9cd25a60f61311b4ad20a819961b5b","body":{"detail":"heap_bytes reads JS_ComputeMemoryUsage (malloc_size), which walks the heap, so the engine samples it once per wait on the host and at the end, not per allocation. Exact tracking needs a counting allocator, and rquickjs's Allocator trait is an unsafe trait, which the workspace's unsafe_code = forbid rules out. When the limit is hit the peak is reported as the limit.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":656},"end":{"line":661},"content_hash":"4581e97cbd67e2311f19b97c4a53d4cfa39584f0274d4c151e95e1c3f0633b84"},"summary":"mem_peak_bytes is sampled, not tracked: memory taken and freed between samples is not seen","tags":["session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot-js/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-21T23:54:25.912565Z","id":"7f585d56dc4bbb5d697c9298a86eb151df14306a7c6cd1fa10c5eb56dc5a0ef9","body":{"detail":"QuickJS refuses an allocation over JS_SetMemoryLimit with a catchable InternalError (or throws null when it cannot allocate that error), so the memory limit ends the run only when the error escapes the script. A V8 isolate in the hosted Worker ends outright, so the two sandboxes differ for a script that catches it; the conformance suite (crates/clax-bot-sandbox/src/conformance.rs, the_memory_limit_ends_an_allocation_bomb) pins only the uncaught case. Because QuickJS's own OOM throw is null, is_out_of_memory maps every uncaught null to ErrorKind::MemoryLimit.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":309},"end":{"line":313},"content_hash":"804290575f22faec4e4bb17c3488c4c429b825add19143c342de9a43c6f3d489"},"summary":"A script can catch QuickJS's out-of-memory error and go on, and its own uncaught `throw null` reads as MemoryLimit","tags":["session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot-js/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-21T23:54:25.912591Z","id":"f6cf2dee8141dea717e578bf032bf069528b6a8d9f766197e54bac463bcef728","body":{"detail":"QuickJS shows when its job queue is empty with no host call pending, so the engine ends the run as Thrown (\"never settles\"). A Worker Loader isolate cannot see its microtask queue, so the hosted sandbox will need its own rule (a wall-clock bound on idle time) and the two may report differently; tests/sandbox.rs a_promise_that_never_settles_ends_the_script covers QuickJS alone.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":562},"end":{"line":568},"content_hash":"50cb1fe4f2e202afdde711f6730d5e3285124a83be03e9909c9c9d11cd3e1bd1"},"summary":"A script awaiting a promise that never settles ends at once here, but no conformance case pins it","tags":["session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot-js/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-21T23:54:25.912617Z","id":"bd4b6cf53d0fd2f7917aec4b47211dbc40ddc8c8f419abe1642d7226e3b8ed69","body":{"detail":"The caller's loop awaits each Host::call before reading the next request, which keeps call order deterministic across sandboxes (the conformance case calls_reach_the_host_in_the_order_made) and the host simple. Running them concurrently (FuturesUnordered over the requests) would cut wall time for scripts that fan out reads.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":179},"end":{"line":194},"content_hash":"83fd6250dfbbcd85fc0d9d0ef0e77db4a353e44589c37e76aea2f03fce4a19f5"},"summary":"Host calls run one at a time, in the order made, even under Promise.all","tags":["revisit:runs spend most of their wall time waiting on independent host calls made together","session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot-js/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-21T23:54:25.912642Z","id":"2b46a01f7c4e5a6f01c7263ce622b2b836e174651b00319bb433bc47c78bf073","body":{"detail":"A thread per script keeps the QuickJS runtime (not Send without rquickjs's parallel feature) on one thread for its whole life and lets the thread CPU clock measure that script alone. A small pool of engine threads would save the spawn per run.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":163},"end":{"line":169},"content_hash":"376ce8d74356849da5e04668658632c2a5ef44a3debc8333164fc8da945fc26a"},"summary":"Each run starts an OS thread of its own instead of taking one from a pool","tags":["revisit:script runs per second on one daemon reach the hundreds, or thread spawn shows in a profile","session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot-js/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-22T01:31:52.270483Z","id":"0caa0e10109d5e41e4e65a7420ca34e7a51ac669717abb222303a5d3b9b171c0","body":{"detail":"The patched QuickJS (third_party/rquickjs-sys.patch) calls the interrupt handler every 100 back-edges and calls, so one builtin call (a sort of a large array, a repeat of a long string) runs to its end between polls. Measured on macOS with cpu_ms 100: the 300-iteration sort loop stops at 106 ms of CPU, the 2000-iteration 'ab'.repeat(5e6).indexOf loop at 308 ms (conformance cases the_cpu_limit_ends_a_loop_of_heavy_*). The watchdog here returns CpuLimit once the engine has been busy for cpu_ms x 4 (at least 500 ms) of wall time, and abandons the thread: it is told to stop, but goes on until its current builtin returns, holding up to memory_bytes and a core; QuickJs::with_abandon_cap (default 8) refuses new scripts with Unavailable while that many are still running. Where the platform has no thread CPU clock, ThreadCpuClock falls back to wall time, which charges a loaded machine's preemption to the script. A run stopped from outside reports busy wall time as cpu_us (an upper bound). Linux has not run these cases.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":404},"end":{"line":451},"content_hash":"51f8606f94ae3f5994fc43a648144f854e652e402f5635133480cb622ee2e170"},"summary":"The CPU budget is enforced at polls every 100 steps and by a wall-clock watchdog; an abandoned engine thread keeps burning CPU until its next poll","supersedes":"6fc3621234e568b808a37fbbd32398d59a3ba096646a75fbea60c7db148c4084","tags":["session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot-js/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-22T01:31:52.289551Z","id":"df8a847ff890876e67488fdea63673736432e67c2c5f1767cade294602429878","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:3cbc0243","summary":"Out of memory now ends the script uncatchably (third_party/rquickjs-sys.patch) and is decided by probing the runtime's own state, not by the thrown value: conformance cases the_memory_limit_cannot_be_caught, a_thrown_null_is_a_thrown_value and a_look_alike_out_of_memory_error_is_a_thrown_value pass (bazelisk test //crates/clax-bot-js:integration --nocache_test_results: PASSED, 10 of 10 runs)","supersedes":"7f585d56dc4bbb5d697c9298a86eb151df14306a7c6cd1fa10c5eb56dc5a0ef9","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot-js/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-22T01:31:52.308449Z","id":"546500721be845f6be9befe4f47b94944b5d161c2469b559cb2dc83ab65aec2f","body":{"detail":"heap_bytes reads JS_ComputeMemoryUsage (malloc_size), which walks the heap, so the engine samples it, plus the bytes of queued host-call arguments, once per wait on the host and at the end. Exact tracking needs a counting allocator, and rquickjs's Allocator trait is an unsafe trait, which the workspace's unsafe_code = forbid rules out. When the limit is hit the peak is reported as the limit. Billing charges memory_bytes, so the figure is informational (crates/clax-bot-sandbox/src/lib.rs, \"Cancellation and metering\").","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1080},"end":{"line":1083},"content_hash":"20b32d4ac4786f7e6e1641b6466acbf2d5fbde3976e059af6cc8ec174efe8311"},"summary":"mem_peak_bytes is sampled, not tracked: memory taken and freed between samples is not seen","supersedes":"6785a4671017f5428b94a43f9381ddfd2c9cd25a60f61311b4ad20a819961b5b","tags":["session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot-js/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-22T01:31:52.327941Z","id":"660f2c2ea57a7b88bca06369b194920e184d5adf787cb2d11179cd7bd034ed35","body":{"detail":"QuickJS shows when its job queue is empty with no host call queued, so the engine ends the run as Thrown (\"never settles\"). A Worker Loader isolate cannot see its microtask queue, so the hosted sandbox will need its own rule (a wall-clock bound on idle time) and the two may report differently; tests/sandbox.rs a_promise_that_never_settles_ends_the_script covers QuickJS alone.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":912},"end":{"line":917},"content_hash":"9b205cb53769edf70be03c2920ac93bf03ebbc5a7da318ded8251524d4349940"},"summary":"A script awaiting a promise that never settles ends at once here, but no conformance case pins it","supersedes":"f6cf2dee8141dea717e578bf032bf069528b6a8d9f766197e54bac463bcef728","tags":["session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot-js/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-22T01:31:52.348055Z","id":"5f6fd2e2c90a4c089f24096b3af0252149b65272bafbac081597a903b02b249d","body":{"detail":"The engine sends a queued call only once the script has nothing left to run, and the caller awaits each Host::call before the next, which keeps call order deterministic across sandboxes (the conformance case calls_reach_the_host_in_the_order_made), lets a call not awaited by the end never be made, and keeps the host simple. Running independent calls concurrently would cut wall time for scripts that fan out reads.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":452},"end":{"line":490},"content_hash":"e47b3ed1cb7d0f18bc54d6414ea80d8c848832e66d7bed0a1af47354b23d7931"},"summary":"Host calls run one at a time, in the order made, even under Promise.all","supersedes":"bd4b6cf53d0fd2f7917aec4b47211dbc40ddc8c8f419abe1642d7226e3b8ed69","tags":["revisit:runs spend most of their wall time waiting on independent host calls made together","session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot-js/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-22T01:31:52.366966Z","id":"c77c16e160bd21a69944a1345dafcb190126a2f83be150e83dc6ecf39d107886","body":{"detail":"A thread per script keeps the QuickJS runtime (not Send without rquickjs's parallel feature) on one thread for its whole life, lets the thread CPU clock measure that script alone, and lets the watchdog abandon a stuck engine without losing a pooled worker. A small pool of engine threads would save the spawn per run.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":386},"end":{"line":392},"content_hash":"3292ba76df91988d2023c292bc5f14d92a6dfa0a6953c4725f892a6d60934942"},"summary":"Each run starts an OS thread of its own instead of taking one from a pool","supersedes":"2b46a01f7c4e5a6f01c7263ce622b2b836e174651b00319bb433bc47c78bf073","tags":["revisit:script runs per second on one daemon reach the hundreds, or thread spawn shows in a profile","session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot-js/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-22T02:03:17.470104Z","id":"daaf00ed99da31ada9cd0a2358f158c593b3e57343cad2088399d7f82c1a936a","body":{"detail":"A thread per script keeps the QuickJS runtime (not Send without rquickjs's parallel feature) on one thread for its whole life, lets the thread CPU clock measure that script alone, and lets the watchdog abandon a stuck engine without losing a pooled worker. A small pool of engine threads would save the spawn per run.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":411},"end":{"line":417},"content_hash":"3292ba76df91988d2023c292bc5f14d92a6dfa0a6953c4725f892a6d60934942"},"summary":"Each run starts an OS thread of its own instead of taking one from a pool","supersedes":"c77c16e160bd21a69944a1345dafcb190126a2f83be150e83dc6ecf39d107886","tags":["revisit:script runs per second on one daemon reach the hundreds, or thread spawn shows in a profile","session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot-js/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-22T02:03:17.500401Z","id":"b06f2008d10427ed5a859529525d7405611b87dc6203c2f4a91790e075db3911","body":{"detail":"The patched QuickJS (third_party/rquickjs-sys.patch, built as third_party/clax-rquickjs-sys) calls the interrupt handler every 100 back-edges and calls, so one builtin call (a sort of a large array, a repeat of a long string) runs to its end between polls. Measured on macOS with cpu_ms 100: the 300-iteration sort loop stops at about 106 ms of CPU, by the engine's own poll (tests/sandbox.rs the_engine_poll_stops_a_loop_of_heavy_builtins), the 2000-iteration 'ab'.repeat(5e6).indexOf loop at about 308 ms. The watchdog here returns CpuLimit once the engine has been busy for cpu_ms x 4 (at least 500 ms) of wall time, and abandons the thread: it is told to stop, but goes on until its current builtin returns, holding up to memory_bytes and a core, CPU that cpu_us (the engine's last figure, what is billed) does not count and busy_us bounds. QuickJs::with_abandon_cap (default 8) refuses new scripts with Unavailable while that many are still running; per-person concurrency is the driver's. Where the platform has no thread CPU clock, ThreadCpuClock falls back to wall time. Linux has not run these cases.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":429},"end":{"line":476},"content_hash":"5fa8dd97340a37b7bb855873cbbd5124144f8e0e4abfb9bf2331a5282b3704b6"},"summary":"The CPU budget is enforced at polls every 100 steps and by a wall-clock watchdog; an abandoned engine thread keeps burning CPU until its next poll","supersedes":"0caa0e10109d5e41e4e65a7420ca34e7a51ac669717abb222303a5d3b9b171c0","tags":["session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot-js/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-22T02:03:17.531650Z","id":"8e0378c20c5e8bb774dc569f3f46dc60702650eb8a79b8e7507d3dcba63d3e34","body":{"detail":"The engine sends a queued call only once the script has nothing left to run, and the caller awaits each Host::call before the next, which keeps call order deterministic across sandboxes (the conformance case calls_reach_the_host_in_the_order_made), lets a call not awaited by the end never be made, and keeps the host simple. Running independent calls concurrently would cut wall time for scripts that fan out reads.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":478},"end":{"line":516},"content_hash":"e47b3ed1cb7d0f18bc54d6414ea80d8c848832e66d7bed0a1af47354b23d7931"},"summary":"Host calls run one at a time, in the order made, even under Promise.all","supersedes":"5f6fd2e2c90a4c089f24096b3af0252149b65272bafbac081597a903b02b249d","tags":["revisit:runs spend most of their wall time waiting on independent host calls made together","session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot-js/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-22T02:03:17.562672Z","id":"44d772af29d5d907d94c3c90edcce2984629bd23241bffe1efda1fdcca65792a","body":{"detail":"QuickJS shows when its job queue is empty with no host call queued, so the engine ends the run as Thrown (\"never settles\"). A Worker Loader isolate cannot see its microtask queue, so the hosted sandbox will need its own rule (a wall-clock bound on idle time) and the two may report differently; tests/sandbox.rs a_promise_that_never_settles_ends_the_script covers QuickJS alone.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":946},"end":{"line":951},"content_hash":"9b205cb53769edf70be03c2920ac93bf03ebbc5a7da318ded8251524d4349940"},"summary":"A script awaiting a promise that never settles ends at once here, but no conformance case pins it","supersedes":"660f2c2ea57a7b88bca06369b194920e184d5adf787cb2d11179cd7bd034ed35","tags":["session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot-js/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-22T02:03:17.597995Z","id":"63c36f1be19635105ed5b7d65ab0f9f984b106834d946e26b71376cd002ee65c","body":{"detail":"heap_bytes reads JS_ComputeMemoryUsage (malloc_size), which walks the heap, so the engine samples it, plus the bytes of queued host-call arguments, once per wait on the host and at the end. Exact tracking needs a counting allocator, and rquickjs's Allocator trait is an unsafe trait, which the workspace's unsafe_code = forbid rules out. When the limit is hit the peak is reported as the limit. Billing charges memory_bytes, so the figure is informational (crates/clax-bot-sandbox/src/lib.rs, \"Cancellation and metering\").","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1114},"end":{"line":1117},"content_hash":"20b32d4ac4786f7e6e1641b6466acbf2d5fbde3976e059af6cc8ec174efe8311"},"summary":"mem_peak_bytes is sampled, not tracked: memory taken and freed between samples is not seen","supersedes":"546500721be845f6be9befe4f47b94944b5d161c2469b559cb2dc83ab65aec2f","tags":["session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot-js/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-22T03:53:22.877410Z","id":"6959c303394c5bae899f2c8bf5ef61afd093757ba7c973f115b8f7f661f103bb","body":{"detail":"QuickJS shows when its job queue is empty with no host call queued, so the engine ends the run as Thrown (\"never settles\"). A Worker Loader isolate cannot see its microtask queue, so the hosted sandbox will need its own rule (a wall-clock bound on idle time) and the two may report differently; tests/sandbox.rs a_promise_that_never_settles_ends_the_script covers QuickJS alone.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":950},"end":{"line":955},"content_hash":"9b205cb53769edf70be03c2920ac93bf03ebbc5a7da318ded8251524d4349940"},"summary":"A script awaiting a promise that never settles ends at once here, but no conformance case pins it","supersedes":"44d772af29d5d907d94c3c90edcce2984629bd23241bffe1efda1fdcca65792a","tags":["session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot-js/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-22T03:53:22.897132Z","id":"14487031f8638352761620f2152cfe9f5ca8990b3ee596696c1a5a89fa489f2b","body":{"detail":"heap_bytes reads JS_ComputeMemoryUsage (malloc_size), which walks the heap, so the engine samples it, plus the bytes of queued host-call arguments, once per wait on the host and at the end. Exact tracking needs a counting allocator, and rquickjs's Allocator trait is an unsafe trait, which the workspace's unsafe_code = forbid rules out. When the limit is hit the peak is reported as the limit. Billing charges memory_bytes, so the figure is informational (crates/clax-bot-sandbox/src/lib.rs, \"Cancellation and metering\").","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1118},"end":{"line":1121},"content_hash":"20b32d4ac4786f7e6e1641b6466acbf2d5fbde3976e059af6cc8ec174efe8311"},"summary":"mem_peak_bytes is sampled, not tracked: memory taken and freed between samples is not seen","supersedes":"63c36f1be19635105ed5b7d65ab0f9f984b106834d946e26b71376cd002ee65c","tags":["session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
